description: Rules for the core layer (domain, useCases, infrastructure)
globs: src/core/**/*
alwaysApply: false
---

# Core Layer

Pure TypeScript. Zero React dependencies.

## domain/

- **entities/**: classes with business behavior and invariants
- **ports/**: interfaces for external dependencies (repositories, services)
- **validators/**: `Validator.ts` for input validation (required, email, etc.)

Validations that affect business operations belong in use cases using `Validator`.
Do not validate business rules in UI.

```typescript
// ✅ Port in domain
interface UserRepository {
  getAll(): Promise<User[]>
}

// ❌ Never import infrastructure here
import { HttpUserRepository } from '../infrastructure/...'
```

## useCases/

- One class per use case, one file per class
- Constructor receives ports via interfaces, never concrete adapters
- Orchestrates domain logic and validation; no HTTP, localStorage, or DOM
- Use `Validator` before persisting or executing side effects

```typescript
class CreateUser {
  private readonly userRepository: UserRepository

  constructor(userRepository: UserRepository) {
    this.userRepository = userRepository
  }

  async execute(input: CreateUserInput): Promise<User> {
    const emailError = Validator.email(input.email, 'Email')
    if (emailError) throw new ValidationError([emailError])
    // ...
  }
}
```

- Class name: verb + entity, no `UseCase` suffix (`GetUsers`, `CreateUser`)
- File name matches class: `GetUsers.ts`, `CreateUser.ts`

## infrastructure/

- **http/**: `HttpClient` (axios) implementing `HttpClientInterface` — swap here to change fetch/axios
- **mappers/**: transform API responses to domain entities
- **repositories/**: implement ports from `domain/ports/`, depend on `HttpClientInterface` (not concrete `HttpClient`) for testability
- May import external libs (axios) — never imported by `domain/` or `useCases/`
